    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3278.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 656,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical Properties of Water\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The flowing water we drink, swim in, and use for rain and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam.\n\n**Other remarkable physical traits include:**\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry vital nutrients through our bloodstreams and minerals through plant roots.\n*   **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on water, allowing marine life to survive under frozen lakes during winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n*   **Oceans:** About 96.5% of Earth's water is held in the oceans as saltwater.\n*   **Freshwater:** Only about 2.5% of Earth’s water is freshwater. Most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and streams.\n\nWater is constantly moving through a closed system called the **water cycle** (or hydrological cycle). It evaporates from the oceans into the sky, forms clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows back into the oceans via rivers.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nAs far as scientists know, **life cannot exist without water.** \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made of about 60% water. It lubricates joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. \n*   **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species of plants and animals, ranging from microscopic plankton to blue whales.\n*   **Agriculture and Industry:** Humans rely on water to grow crops, generate electricity (hydroelectric power), manufacture goods, and cook.\n\nIn summary, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with complex, miraculous properties that make existence possible."
